Various compiler transformations for irregular recursive structures have been developed in recent years; however, they focus on locality. In contrast, recursive data structure programs suffer acutely from pointer-chasing delays leading to low memory-level parallelism (MLP) and also making prefetchers ineffective at hiding latency. This paper presents RIST (Recursive Iteration Space Tiling), a transformation that addresses the pointer-chasing bottleneck, thereby systematically enhancing MLP across structured mutual recursion. RIST generalizes classical loop tiling to recursive programs by batching independent recursive iterations and advancing them concurrently. It restructures recursive traversals to expose cross-instance parallelism hidden by pointer chasing. When cross-traversal dependences have bounded distance, RIST applies a loop-skewing-type pre-pass to enable legal tiling. Further, we extend compiler support beyond nested recursion to structured mutual recursion. We present Continuation-Preserving Multi-Phase Scheduling (CP-MPS) that models structured mutual recursion, and enables legality of RIST transformation. This enables compiler optimization of previously intractable recursive programs. At a tile size of 5, RIST delivers up to 260% performance improvement over O3-optimized binaries on Intel i9-14900 CPU. When combined with software prefetching, the gains increase further, reaching up to 280% on the i9-14900 and 235% on the i7-7700.